WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.237
Time: 4 minutes.

00:00:01.477 --> 00:00:06.064
A record is useful when someone can determine whether the problem has been resolved.

00:00:06.304 --> 00:00:09.973
Replace ‘works correctly’ with observable acceptance criteria.

00:00:10.213 --> 00:00:25.182
For this sample: export DEMO-7; confirm raw CSV preserves 00127 and 00008 exactly; confirm an ordinary alphanumeric code A127 remains unchanged; confirm

00:00:25.182 --> 00:00:28.453
the row count matches the source.

00:00:28.693 --> 00:00:33.408
These are training criteria, not vendor-defined fields or product requirements.

00:00:34.098 --> 00:00:37.981
Work with the team’s triage process to set ownership and priority.

00:00:38.221 --> 00:00:42.658
Do not use the highest priority merely because the requester is frustrated.

00:00:42.898 --> 00:00:47.975
Record impact and urgency so the authorized triager can decide consistently.

00:00:48.215 --> 00:00:54.359
If you are not authorized to assign or change priority, provide the evidence to the responsible person.

00:00:55.049 --> 00:00:58.911
Worked handoff: ‘Reproduced in demo 2.4 with DEMO-7.

00:00:59.151 --> 00:01:01.348
Raw CSV drops leading zeros.

00:01:01.588 --> 00:01:03.081
Acceptance checks listed.

00:01:03.321 --> 00:01:09.145
Awaiting triage owner.’ This says what is known and who needs to act without promising a delivery date.

00:01:09.835 --> 00:01:15.446
Before saving, confirm the space, work type, title, required fields, and access context.

00:01:15.686 --> 00:01:23.110
A work item key identifies the record; use that key in further discussions rather than creating disconnected copies.

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:29.089
Do not assume that mentioning a person assigns ownership or guarantees they have seen the request.

00:01:29.779 --> 00:01:30.867
Now it's your turn.

00:01:31.107 --> 00:01:33.411
Which acceptance criterion is testable?

00:01:33.651 --> 00:01:36.765
Pause the video and make your choice in the activity below.

